   ['Effective managers establish routines early (entering, distributing materials, group work, finishing), set a few positively stated rules with the class, and teach them explicitly. Jacob Kounin described "withitness": the teacher\'s awareness of everything happening in the room, shown by acting early before problems grow, along with smooth transitions and keeping the whole group busy.',
    'Positive discipline focuses on teaching appropriate behaviour. Teachers praise specific good behaviour, use proximity, eye contact and the learner\'s name to redirect quietly, and apply logical consequences linked to the behaviour (a learner who damages a book repairs or replaces it). Restorative conversations ask what happened, who was affected and how to put it right.',
    'Corporal punishment causes physical and emotional harm, damages relationships, and is associated with fear, absenteeism and drop-out; it does not teach self-control. It is banned in schools in Kenya (since 2001, reinforced by the 2010 Constitution and the Children Act), South Africa (Schools Act 1996) and many other countries, and Ghana\'s education service has moved to positive discipline guidelines. Persistent misbehaviour may signal learning difficulties, hunger or problems at home, which call for support and referral.'],
